hi there steve kaufman here um you know  i recently put up a video  and this is steve kaufman of course of  the linguist on language blog  founder of link and so forth  but i put up a video where i said the  only language we need is english  and i think most people understood that  i did that uh tongue-in-cheek  although i did get some reaction from  people who disagreed with that's the  title  of the video and didn't listen to the  video and in the video i explained how  my languages had been useful to me in my  business career  but but why do we learn languages  um  you know we often hear  that explanations like we should learn  uh you know english because it's the  international language of commerce  french because it's the language of  of uh diplomacy or in canada because  it's one of our official languages we  should learn french  we should learn chinese because all of  our kids are going to be doing business  in chinese in the future  guarantee them a job kind of thing  learn latin because it'll help you with  all your languages learn  german or greek because  it'll help you with medical terms and on  and on all of these so-called  utilitarian reasons for learning  languages  frankly i think those reasons are  basically not valid  with the possible exception of english  where english  is probably the most useful  language to learn because it has become  the dominant language of  international trade and science and and  that's not about to change in the near  term  however even for english the vast  majority of people who learn english  will not use it very much  in their job they won't use it to talk  to their neighbors if you live in japan  russia brazil  even germany  china  korea spain  indonesia i mean you don't have a daily  need to use english  a small number of people work for  companies that are involved in  international trade and they need  english  um  the other languages  it's basically not true that french is  the language of diplomacy that german  helps you in medical school and none of  that stuff is true  i did a bit of latin at school i was  basically i've forgotten it mostly and i  think was quite irrelevant in so far as  uh you know my language study is  concerned yeah one romance language  helps you with the next so if you learn  french that makes spanish easier makes  portuguese easier and so forth but latin  you don't need it some people say learn  esperanto because it's the language of  peace or something no if you're  interested in esperanto learn it but  don't don't apply to it some external  utilitarian motive i don't i don't  personally believe in all of that  the main reason to learn  languages  any foreign language is because it's  tremendously  personally rewarding  brings you pleasure and happiness  uh  not only does it bring you happiness it  also  gives other people happiness because  whereas english-speaking people are used  to the idea that people speak english  speak their language take it for granted  in fact most speakers of other languages  don't take it for granted and they're  typically quite pleased to hear you  speak their language it's a sign of  respect towards their language which is  most people basically appreciate that  and even in english if we hear someone  from another country who speaks english  very well we appreciate it we respect it  and we appreciate it it makes us feel  good but most of all  we ourselves feel good if we can speak  other languages and if i take as an  example  you know the languages that i've been  working on for the last 10 years  cantonese korean russian portuguese  and even where i've been refreshing my  italian or german or spanish  it's been largely out of personal  interest out of the pleasure that i  derive from those languages uh just the  last week i've been working on italian  so i went to el nadatore  dot com and downloaded  an audiobook of ulysses in italian  uh  marie color del vino  um  a wonderful story just wonderful and to  hear ulysses told in a slightly  simplified version i think it perhaps  might be intended for children or  teenagers but in italian  uh  and and you feel closer to the  mediterranean world because it's in  italian a wonderful experience um  you know it's be staying with il  naratore  moro silo which is mauricio  he has done a version of pinocchio  which you can buy on his site and at  which i have and which is  just phenomenal he  uh basically uh uh uses different voices  for the different  characters in the story and of course we  have the text at link so that i can also  read it there and save any words and  phrases that i need  i've also been reading a book in italian  by alberto moravia so i've had this  italian experience for a week and i've  improved my italian plus i've been just  enjoying it tremendously  and it doesn't matter what stage you're  at i enjoyed my russian right from the  very beginning  so to me therefore the reason to learn  languages is because it is very  enjoyable and that should be the  emphasis in schools  and if uh say in canada every english  canadian english-speaking canadian is  obliged to learn french and people  resent that in many cases aside from the  fact that it's taught in a very  uninteresting way  but let people have the choice nowadays  there's so many resources  available on the internet  uh  for any language basically  not only content at different levels but  explanations of grammar  dictionaries lots of things that can be  downloaded into an ipod what the  teachers need to do they don't need all  we don't need experts in these different  languages we need an expert in language  learning in the school to coordinate the  activities of learners to show them how  they can discover languages and have fun  and how they can use uh all of these  resources to succeed rather than having  them sit in class and and and you know  fight their way through some text that's  assigned to them in the languages  assigned to them it it all sort of  contributes to taking the fun out of  language learning so the conclusion is  why do we learn other languages because  it's tremendously enjoyable for  ourselves and for other people and what  better reason to do something than  because it's enjoyable and in fact i  just thought of something there it's  very similar to musical instruments  um a very small number of people learn  musical instruments because  they're going to be professional  performance that's a performers it's a  very small number the vast majority  learn musical instruments because it's  rewarding it's fun it's enjoyable it's  enjoyable for them as individuals and  it's durable enjoyable  hopefully for other people who are  listening or who are dancing or who are  singing along with the music so  we learn languages for the same reason  that we learn  to play musical instruments it's because  it's tremendously enjoyable thank you  very much for listening
 

